===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[great#Adjective|greatest]] [[possible]] [[capability]], [[extent]], or [[quantity]]; [[maximum#Noun|maximum]].
#: {{synonyms|en|yondermost}}
#: {{ux|en|at the '''utmost'''&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; to the '''utmost'''}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|edition=5th|page=116|passage=[...] I am reſolved to pluck up the heart of a man, and to try my '''utmoſt''' to get from under his hand.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:James Tyrrell (writer)|James Tyrrell]]|chapter=Book XII. Containing the Reign of [[w:Richard II of England|King ''Richard'' II]].|title=The General History of England, both Ecclesiastical and Civil:{{nb...|Containing the Reign of Richard II. Taken from the Most Antient Records, Manuscripts, and Printed Historians. With an Appendix, being a Brief and Impartial Disquisition into that Great Question, whether the Commons of England had Ever Any Other Representatives in Parliament, than the Tenants in Capite before the 49th of Henry the Third? Wherein All the Arguments that have been Publish’d in Several Treatises on this Subject, are Fairly Represented; together with Such Answers and Replies as can be Given to Them.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} W. Rogers,{{nb...|in Fleetstreet}}; J. Taylor,{{nb...|in St. Paul’s Church-Yard}}; J. Sprint,{{nb...|in Little-Britain}}; and A[ndrew] Bell,{{nb...|in Cornhill.}}|year=1704|volume=III, 2nd part|page=912|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gh1DAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA912|oclc=1181415101|passage=[T]he '''utmoſt''' they could do, was to perſuade Him to lay aſide all Rancor and Diſcontent againſt thoſe Confederate Lords, and to hearken to their deſires.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=122–123|pageref=123|passage=But Scrooge was all the worse for this. It thrilled him with a vague uncertain horror, to know that behind the dusky shroud there were ghostly eyes intently fixed upon him, while he, though he stretched his own to the '''utmost''', could see nothing but a spectral hand and one great heap of black.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Meeting Point|page=232|passage=Meanwhile Nanny Broome was recovering from her initial panic and seemed anxious to make up for any kudos she might have lost, by exerting her personality to the '''utmost'''. She took the policeman's helmet and placed it on a chair, and unfolded his tunic to shake it and fold it up again for him.}}

